{
  "version": 1,
  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "t41",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "excerpt": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "text": "clarify",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-07T22:12:14.764Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-07T22:13:39.767Z",
          "html": "<p>What T41 means, in plain words:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    A library (say, a cards library) can make things of its own kinds: its deck makes cards with <code>a new card</code>.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    When Solitaire uses that library, you'd want the deck to make <em>Solitaire's</em> card (with Solitaire's own looks and\n    rules), not the library's plain one.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    Since P26 that happens, but only when the library's project file lists the type in its <code>exports</code>:\n    <code>\"exports\": [\"Card\", \"Deck\"]</code>. Without that line, the deck keeps making the library's own plain cards.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    I chose that so no existing project's compiled code changed. Making it the rule for every type would have changed 5\n    files in recipe-box, recetario, live-room and sala-en-vivo, for no gain.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The follow-up question T41 holds: is \"only what <code>exports</code> lists\" the rule you want, or should a library always let the project using it supply its own version of each type?</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Nothing changes unless you pick the other rule;  then every library type is made by name, and those 5 files' compiled code changes.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "p-one-ending#field-4",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "One Ending",
      "excerpt": "The shape, from the text. One function, SP.formOfText() , holds the rule; SpellF",
      "text": "<device-database>",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-09T23:39:25.102Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "<database-on-this-device>",
      "offset": 2957,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-09T23:59:58.200Z",
          "html": "<p>Agreed that <code>database</code> is the wrong word: it implies SQL, and these are the browser's own stores. You chose, in the chat:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>&lt;device-storage&gt;</code>: kept for good (<code>localStorage</code>)</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;window-storage&gt;</code>: gone when the tab closes (<code>sessionStorage</code>)</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;memory-storage&gt;</code>: gone when the page goes</li>\n</ul>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>The plan says these three tags. The plain lines become <code>is stored on this device</code> and <code>is stored in memory</code>, and the old <code>database</code> lines keep reading.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "p-one-ending#field-4",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "One Ending",
      "excerpt": "The shape, from the text. One function, SP.formOfText() , holds the rule; SpellF",
      "text": "<atmosphere-account personal> ?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-09T23:39:42.994Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "<personal-atmosphere-account",
      "offset": 2866,
      "edited": "2026-11-09T23:39:47.627Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-10T00:00:00.289Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes, and shorter still, as you said in the chat: <code>&lt;atmosphere&gt;</code> is an account, <code>&lt;atmosphere personal&gt;</code> each person's own, <code>&lt;atmosphere space&gt;</code> a space.</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>One tag and two bare flags, the way <code>notebook</code> is a flag on a page. The plain lines don't change.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c4": {
      "anchor": "p-one-ending#field-4",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "One Ending",
      "excerpt": "The shape, from the text. One function, SP.formOfText() , holds the rule; SpellF",
      "text": "let's reconsider this -- what are the top-level tags we have now that could be shortened?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-09T23:40:48.171Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "spell-view>)",
      "offset": 5277,
      "edited": "2026-11-09T23:40:53.973Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-10T00:00:02.285Z",
          "html": "<p>These can lose their prefix: <code>&lt;page&gt;</code> (410 uses), <code>&lt;extend&gt;</code> (51, today <code>more-of</code>), <code>&lt;view&gt;</code> (36), <code>&lt;app&gt;</code> (28), <code>&lt;thing&gt;</code> (27), <code>&lt;site&gt;</code> (3), <code>&lt;property-editor&gt;</code> (1).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>You chose a phase of its own after this one: Short Tags.</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;spell-description&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;spell-comment&gt;</code> become one tag, <code>&lt;note&gt;</code>.</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;spell&gt;</code>, the block of code, keeps its name: it's the language's name, not a prefix.</li>\n</ul>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>This phase leaves template tags alone. Short Tags changes them, and the old spellings keep reading.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c5": {
      "anchor": "p-one-ending#field-4",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "One Ending",
      "excerpt": "The shape, from the text. One function, SP.formOfText() , holds the rule; SpellF",
      "text": "when is this used?  <json-file>, <text-file> etc?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-09T23:41:35.778Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "<file>",
      "offset": 2407,
      "edited": "2026-11-09T23:41:43.744Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-10T00:00:04.935Z",
          "html": "<p><code>&lt;file&gt;</code> is one JSON5 file on a server, holding each collection's rows. No project uses it, only parser tests.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>As you said in the chat, <code>file</code> stops meaning that: it's <code>&lt;json-file&gt;</code> and <code>is a json file at</code>, and plain <code>is a file at</code> says to write <code>json file</code>.</li>\n  <li><code>&lt;text-file&gt;</code>: not planned. A place holds rows, and plain text has none, so it needs someone to say what a row is first.</li>\n</ul>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>The plan renames this kind to <code>json file</code>. No <code>text file</code> kind is added.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "on": {}
}
